  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Staying humid with periods of showers/scattered \nthunderstorms with heavy downpours and the potential for \nlocalized flash flooding each afternoon/evening through at least\nSaturday\n\nTarget area for convection will be near or just to the south of\nthe southern edge of 30-40NM wide band of 2+ inch rains that\nfell this past Sunday and where Flash Flood guidance is the\nlowest in our CWA or only 1.5-2.0 inches/3 hours. That being\nsaid the bulk of the heaviest rain and tallest TSRA should\ngravitate toward the best llvl convergence and slightly cooler\nmid-level temps - located across the Lower and Middle Susq\nValley and where current SB CAPE has increased to between\n2500-3000 J/KG. \n\nAs noted earlier, MBE vectors are the shortest across this\nregion in the 21Z Thu-02Z Friday timeframe, suggesting the \npotential for training cells and locally heavy rain.\n\nThe overall sfc pattern remains pretty much unchanged from \nyesterday with very humid (dewpoints 70-75F) air in place and a \nhigh off the coast. \n\nAlso similar to yesterday, the bulk of the convection (mainly\nacross the Se 1/2 of our CWA should dissipate after 03Z Friday\nwith a transition to areas of fog/stratus after midnight,\nespecially in areas that receive rainfall. \n\nPrevious Disc...\n\nThe deep layer moisture (PWAT) will be just the slightest bit \nlower, though, mainly in the W - with 2\"+ amounts holding over \nthe E. Also, the flow aloft will be a little more progressive \nwith storm motions about 260 (from the W to the E) at 30 MPH. We\nstill are low on confidence of the placement of the heaviest of\nrain. It didn't rain heavily in the SE Wed, and that is where \nthe best signal for heavy rain seems to be today. FFG is lowest \nin the Laurels and also in a stripe from Lewistown up to \nLaPorte, and higher elsewhere. At the same time, the initial \nstorms will probably fire up on the Alleghenies where they did \nhave the heaviest rain yesterday. Consensus among local offices \nis that we don't have enough to compel us to post any flood \nwatches with this forecast package. \n\nThe flow aloft does get more zonal (W-E) Sat which should keep \nany storms moving along a little faster. Thus, the flooding \nthreat may also decrease despite a continuation of high sfc \ndewpoints. The case for flooding concerns for Sat are mainly\nthanks to an accumulative effect over the previous days. Sunday\nactually looks like we may be rain-free with slightly lower \ndewpoints, but that is a trend at this point and not a \ncertainty. As the flow becomes faster aloft for next week, we \nwill see multiple chances for showers and thunderstorms. \n\n   ------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Severe thunderstorms are possible today and \nagain Friday afternoon/evening\n\nDeep moisture will make some tall storms today and the modest \n30 kts of deep layer shear might be enough to make a severe \ngust or two. The higher threat is SE of an AOO-SEG-AVP line. A\nsecond potential, rather narrow ribbon of SHRA/TSRA could\ndevelop near the Western slopes of the Alleghenies and Laurels \nwhere a second weak sfc trough, mid level thermal trough and the\nbest late day heating of those slopes will be found.\n\nThe 5H heights will fall on Friday. A shortwave trough looks \nlike it will roll overhead late in the day/evening, too. The \nPWATs stay 1.75\" W and 2.10\" E, and the cooling mid levels could\nhelp grow the storms and perhaps give them more of a chance to \nbecome severe vs today when we'll have less upper \nforcing/support. SPC MRGL risk for damaging wind gusts covers \ngenerally the same locations today and Friday. Depending on \ntiming of the next shortwave from the west, Sat may be dry (esp \nE), but Sunday holds a better chc for dry weather. A very \nactive/progressive upper flow next week will keep it unsettled \nwith near-daily chcs for SHRA/TSRA. \n \n   ------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Heat index will reach 100F in the far SErn\ncounties today\n\nThe very high (70-75F) sfc dewpoints will keep it HH&H at least\nthrough Sat. Today will probably hold the highest heat index\nvalues. Better sunshine today in the SE will probably boost the\ntemps 4-6F over Wed. The guidance has been consistent painting \ntemps and dews sufficient to make HIs around 100 SE of \nHarrisburg, with the highest possibility of 100+ temps in \nLancaster and Lebanon counties. Went with a heat advy for there \nthrough 8 PM. Friday will probably just 1-2F lower on HI, so\nwe'll keep the advy for today at this point.",
